  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\int_0^1 f(x) \\, d\\lambda = 0\n\\quad \\text{where} \\quad\nf(x) = \\begin{cases} 1/q & \\text{if } x = p/q \\text{ in lowest terms} \\\\ 0 & \\text{if } x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\mathbb{Q} \\end{cases}",
    "plain": "The **Thomae function** (also called the modified Dirichlet function or popcorn function) assigns $1/q$ to every rational $p/q$ in lowest terms, and $0$ to every irrational. Compute its Lebesgue integral explicitly using Mathlib's Bochner integral framework. The answer is 0 because the Thomae function equals 0 almost everywhere — its support is exactly $\\mathbb{Q} \\cap [0,1]$, which is countable and has Lebesgue measure 0.",
